<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umkhonto we Sizwe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1961">1961</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as MK</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Umkhonto we Sizwe (&quot;Spear of the Nation&quot;), or MK, was the armed wing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>, founded in December 1961 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-communist-party">South African Communist Party</a> after the banning of the ANC. Led at first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, it began with sabotage and later waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign from bases in neighbouring countries. It suspended armed struggle in 1990 and joined the national army in 1994.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Umkhonto we Sizwe, meaning Spear of the Nation and abbreviated MK, was the paramilitary wing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> in the wake of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville massacre</a>. Its aim was to bring armed pressure on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> government to abandon apartheid. In June 1961 Mandela warned newspapers that a campaign of sabotage would follow unless the government called a national constitutional convention. In his speech at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rivonia-trial">Rivonia Trial</a> he said that he and colleagues had concluded that violence was inevitable and that it would be wrong to keep preaching non-violence while the government met peaceful demands with force. The first attacks came on 16 December 1961.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>MK set up regional commands in the main centres, and its high command included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-tambo">Oliver Tambo</a>, Walter Sisulu, Govan Mbeki and Joe Slovo. The first target was an electrical substation, followed over a year and a half by attacks on government posts, machines and power facilities. The organisation was banned and classified as a terrorist group by the government, which answered with laws allowing detention without trial. On 11 July 1963 nineteen leaders were arrested at Liliesleaf Farm in Rivonia, its headquarters, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rivonia-trial">Rivonia Trial</a>.</p><p>In the 1980s MK attacked police stations and, in 1980, two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasol">Sasol</a> oil-from-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> plants. From 1985 to 1987 it laid anti-tank mines on rural roads in the Northern Transvaal, a tactic abandoned because of civilian casualties among black labourers. The ANC counted 30 explosions and 23 deaths, the government 57 explosions and 25 deaths. Abroad, MK allied itself with the Zimbabwe People's Revolutionary Army, and a joint force crossing into Rhodesia in August 1967 was largely destroyed in Operation Nickel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>MK drew nearly all its equipment from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-communist-party">South African Communist Party</a>'s contacts, and kept arms depots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a>. The Truth and Reconciliation Commission found that torture and executions without due process were routine in ANC camps, mainly between 1979 and 1989, though not official policy, and called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> bombing a gross violation of human rights. It also noted that, of the three main parties to the conflict, only the ANC committed itself to the Geneva Protocols. MK was formally disbanded at Orlando Stadium in Soweto on 16 December 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “UMkhonto weSizwe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1960 had been followed by a state of emergency and the banning of the African National Congress and the Pan Africanist Congress, and in December 1961 the ANC and the South African Communist Party launched an armed wing, <b>Umkhonto we Sizwe</b>, with Nelson Mandela as its first commander.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Umkhonto we Sizwe</b>, “Spear of the Nation”, announced in December 1961 that the people now faced only two choices, to submit or to fight; it began with sabotage against installations, intended to avoid loss of life, before turning to guerrilla war.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
